 "productionNotes": "The C4 total most often printed is 358,180 for the 1984 through 1996 model years, given by the Corvette Action Center's C4 production page and repeated by a Corvette parts retailer's generation summary. That figure is not carried as this page's total because the year-by-year figures behind it do not agree across sources. The clearest case is 1988: the Corvette Action Center, The Corvette Story and the National Corvette Museum all give 22,789 (15,382 coupes and 7,407 convertibles), while Consumer Guide lists 23,239 under a heading of calendar-year sales. Consumer Guide's label may explain the gap, since a calendar year and a model year are different counting periods, but no source consulted reconciles the two. For 1996 CorvSport lists 17,167 base coupes and 4,369 convertibles, which sum to 21,536, matching Consumer Guide and Greg Wapling's year table at 21,536; Wikipedia's table says 21,537, one car higher. For 1984 every source consulted gives 51,547. The missing 1983 model year carries its own disagreement. History.com and Wikipedia say GM built 43 pre-production pilot cars, of which 42 were crushed and one survives at the National Corvette Museum in Bowling Green, Kentucky. Consumer Guide says 47 cars were built with 1983 serial numbers. None was sold to the public, and the gap between 43 and 47 is not resolved by any source consulted here. Special-edition counts are steadier: 2,050 for the 1988 35th Anniversary package, 1,000 for the 1996 Grand Sport (810 coupes and 190 convertibles) and 5,412 for the 1996 Collector Edition all appear in two or more sources. The 1993 40th Anniversary count of 6,749 rests on a single encyclopedia source. Callaway twin-turbo counts disagree by one car for 1988 (124 against 125). No curb weight figure was found in any source fetched for this page.",
